.TH DATE 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
date \- print the date and time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B date
.RI [ +FORMAT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B date
prints the date and time by the board's real-time clock, which keeps the world's
time: the clock files on disks are stamped with.
.PP
Dates are the world's calendar: days are numbered from 1, and a day has 24 of
the game's hours, as Minecraft counts them, so 06:00 is sunrise and 18:00
sunset.
The first day's sunrise is the epoch.
A day of the world is 20 minutes of real time.
.PP
With no
.IR FORMAT ,
it prints
.BR "Day 3 14:05:09" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI + FORMAT
What to print, as
.B os.date
formats it:
.B %d
the day,
.B %H
.B %M
.B %S
the hour, minute and second,
.B %T
all three,
.B %R
hours and minutes,
.B %s
seconds since the epoch,
.B %c
the default,
.B %%
a percent sign.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when it printed; 1 for a format it does not know.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
date
date +%H:%M
date +%s
.fi
.SH NOTES
GNU
.B date
prints the Gregorian calendar's date, and can set the clock
.RB ( \-s );
mcos's world has no months or years, and its clock is the world's, which no
program sets.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uptime (1)
